WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.280 --> 00:00:03.512
(upbeat music)
(tapping)

00:00:03.512 --> 00:00:05.272
- Dance is something that comes

00:00:05.272 --> 00:00:08.272
in every last one of us, from birth.

00:00:10.011 --> 00:00:13.145
What we do is take that and give them

00:00:13.145 --> 00:00:15.895
an opportunity to personalize it.

00:00:16.789 --> 00:00:19.019
And then it empowers us

00:00:19.019 --> 00:00:21.429
to dance at this level.

00:00:21.429 --> 00:00:25.596
(upbeat music)
(tapping)

00:00:30.478 --> 00:00:32.645
(tapping)

00:00:34.747 --> 00:00:36.877
My name is Melba Mitchell-Ayco,

00:00:36.877 --> 00:00:40.187
and I'm the founder of
Northwest Tap Connection.

00:00:40.187 --> 00:00:44.467
Our mission is to close the gap
for underserved communities,

00:00:44.467 --> 00:00:47.077
with an emphasis on children of color.

00:00:47.077 --> 00:00:50.008
And really just open that
door for young people

00:00:50.008 --> 00:00:52.739
who would not be able
to have accessibility

00:00:52.739 --> 00:00:55.029
to the arts because of cost,

00:00:55.029 --> 00:00:58.453
to have those opportunities
that are enriching.

00:00:58.453 --> 00:01:00.679
When I started Northwest Tap, I was working

00:01:00.679 --> 00:01:02.679
for the Seattle Police Department.

00:01:02.679 --> 00:01:05.479
And my goal was that
children that I got to see

00:01:05.479 --> 00:01:09.762
on a weekly basis, I would
never see across my desk.

00:01:09.762 --> 00:01:13.529
This walkin' peace is very
much about our culture.

00:01:13.529 --> 00:01:15.789
And you need to dance it that way.

00:01:15.789 --> 00:01:19.650
Tap dancing is the foundation
of our organization.

00:01:19.650 --> 00:01:22.902
I strive to make sure
that children understand

00:01:22.902 --> 00:01:27.069
that it is a dance form that
comes out of black culture.

00:01:28.001 --> 00:01:30.202
- Up the beat and up on the end.

00:01:30.202 --> 00:01:31.436
Let me see that.

00:01:31.436 --> 00:01:33.525
- [Melba] It comes out of protest.

00:01:33.525 --> 00:01:36.152
And we have to appreciate it,

00:01:36.152 --> 00:01:39.902
embrace it and protect
it as we pass it down.

00:01:40.889 --> 00:01:43.056
(tapping)

00:01:44.302 --> 00:01:45.412
- Northwest Tap is a race and

00:01:45.412 --> 00:01:47.662
social justice oriented studio,

00:01:47.662 --> 00:01:50.529
and being an African
American male, I don't think,

00:01:50.529 --> 00:01:51.852
had I danced at another studio,

00:01:51.852 --> 00:01:53.379
that I would have been surrounded

00:01:53.379 --> 00:01:55.375
by so many fellow African Americans.

00:01:55.375 --> 00:01:57.335
And I feel like that has given me

00:01:57.335 --> 00:02:00.612
the confidence to feel
comfortable in my skin.

00:02:00.612 --> 00:02:02.309
In normal everyday life,

00:02:02.309 --> 00:02:04.335
I don't really like talking very much.

00:02:04.335 --> 00:02:05.799
But when it comes to dancing,

00:02:05.799 --> 00:02:10.448
I'm able to just communicate
in a way that touches people

00:02:10.448 --> 00:02:13.615
rather than what they're just hearing.

00:02:15.512 --> 00:02:18.225
(speaking faintly)

00:02:18.225 --> 00:02:19.710
- Why?
- My feelings

00:02:19.710 --> 00:02:22.749
as I watch young people dance...

00:02:22.749 --> 00:02:25.685
There is this sense of pride

00:02:25.685 --> 00:02:27.799
for the work that they do.

00:02:27.799 --> 00:02:30.585
But then at the same time,

00:02:30.585 --> 00:02:34.349
there's this sense of
fear and trouble, for me.

00:02:34.349 --> 00:02:38.516
How much longer will we
continue with this struggle?

00:02:40.099 --> 00:02:42.875
Dance is such an empowering tool.

00:02:42.875 --> 00:02:47.348
We are a community of
many colors, many genders,

00:02:47.348 --> 00:02:50.015
identifications, but we are one.

00:02:51.185 --> 00:02:53.795
Through these children that dance,

00:02:53.795 --> 00:02:56.072
we're creating a new tomorrow.

00:02:56.072 --> 00:03:00.239
(upbeat music)
(tapping)

00:03:03.522 --> 00:03:06.439
(electronic music)

